# Sanjeevani patch template: user input used as a printf format string.

name: format string
cwe: CWE-134 externally controlled format string

# printf called with a bare variable as its ONLY argument. A literal format
# string like printf("hi %s\n", x) is fine and must not match, so require the
# argument to be an identifier rather than a quoted string.
detect: \bprintf\s*\(\s*\w+\s*\)

find: printf\s*\(\s*(\w+)\s*\)
replace: printf("%s", \1)

explain: printf treats its first argument as a format string. If that string
 came from the user, "%s" makes it read pointers that were never passed and
 "%n" makes it WRITE to one. Passing the text as an ARGUMENT instead means it
 is printed literally, so "%n" is just two harmless characters.

caveat: none worth noting. This fix preserves output exactly for any input
 containing no format specifiers, and for inputs that do contain them the
 original behaviour was undefined anyway.
